---
import ToolPage from '../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import CardDraw from '../components/tools/CardDraw';
import { getCatalog } from '../lib/content/catalog';
const dealerRules = new Set(getCatalog().map(rule => rule.slug));
const hasDealerRules = ['spades-bicycle-en', 'cribbage-bicycle-en', 'euchre-bicycle-en'].every(slug => dealerRules.has(slug));
---
<ToolPage current="/card-draw/" title="Draw a Card Online: Random Playing Card Generator | Who Goes First?" description="Draw random cards from a shuffled 52-card deck online. Free and fair, with no repeats until you reshuffle. Great for high-card draws to pick a dealer." heading="Draw a card" lede="Draw from a freshly shuffled 52-card deck.">
  <CardDraw client:load />
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>Drawing for the deal</h2>
    {hasDealerRules ? <p>Many card games choose the first dealer by a draw: in <a href="/games/spades-bicycle-en/">Spades</a> the high card deals, in <a href="/games/cribbage-bicycle-en/">Cribbage</a> and <a href="/games/euchre-bicycle-en/">Euchre</a> the low card does. Take turns drawing here, one card each, and compare.</p> : <p>Take turns drawing here, one card each, and compare to decide who deals first.</p>}
    <h2>How the deck works</h2>
    <p>The deck is shuffled with an unbiased Fisher–Yates shuffle using your browser’s secure randomness, and cards don’t come back until you shuffle a new deck, just like a real pack.</p>
    <h2>Running a high-card draw</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Agree before anyone draws whether aces are high or low, and whether high or low card wins. Rulebooks differ, so check yours.</li>
      <li>Pass the device around so each player taps <strong>Draw a card</strong> once. The drawn list keeps every card in order, so no one has to remember.</li>
      <li>If two players tie for the winning rank, only those players draw again from the same deck.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Some games also break ties by suit. Bridge players, for example, often rank spades over hearts, diamonds and clubs when drawing for partners. Use a suit order only if your group agrees to it first.</p>
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <h3>Are jokers included?</h3>
    <p>No. It is a standard 52-card deck: four suits of ace through king.</p>
    <h3>What happens after 52 cards?</h3>
    <p>The deck is empty, just like a real pack. Tap <strong>Shuffle a new deck</strong> to start again with all 52 cards.</p>
    <h3>Can I use it to deal a hand?</h3>
    <p>Yes, for casual play. Draw the cards one at a time and note them. Because the drawn list is visible to everyone at the device, it suits open draws better than hidden hands.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
